<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 1px solid #a7d9ee;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin: 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 25%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 75%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Preserving Our Heritage</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Learning How We Take Care of Our Past</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Narrate a story about personal experiences. Interactive: Engage in storytelling sessions using personal experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can narrate a story about personal experiences with cultural traditions, illustrating their importance for heritage preservation (e.g., "During Corpus Christi celebrations in La Villa de Los Santos, I watched traditional dances that showcased our folkloric heritage.")<br>Can engage in storytelling to describe the personal importance of heritage (e.g., "I visited the Panama Canal, which fills me with pride because my grandfather helped with its construction." "I visited the Panama Canal, where my grandfather worked during its construction.")<br>Can explain the cultural significance of Indigenous heritage by describing traditional practices (e.g., "The Guna people's traditional use of molas, brightly colored hand-woven textiles, is an important expression of their cultural identity and history.")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a collage of images of famous Panamanian heritage sites (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Viejo, San Lorenzo Fort, Indigenous villages like Emberá). Students work in pairs to verbally identify the sites and share one sentence about why they think each place is important. For example, "Casco Viejo is a place where you can see colonial architecture." The teacher encourages the use of previously learned vocabulary such as 'historical,' 'culture,' 'preserve,' 'sites,' 'attracts,' and 'legacy'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using relative clauses and the target vocabulary while pointing to images. For example: "This is Casco Viejo, <b>which</b> is a UNESCO World Heritage Site and <b>attracts</b> many <b>tourists</b>. It has <b>historical</b> buildings <b>where</b> you can experience Panamanian <b>culture</b>." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of the long vowel sound /eɪ/ in words like 'trade,' 'became,' 'great,' 'say,' and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words like 'important' (/ɪmˈpɔː.tənt/) and 'heritage' (/ˈher.ɪ.tɪdʒ/), having students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When I say 'Casco Viejo, which attracts many tourists,' what does 'which' refer to?" (Expected answer: Casco Viejo)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'a village where traditional dances are performed,' what kind of information does 'where' give us?" (Expected answer: It tells us the location/place for the dances)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short audio clip (1-2 minutes) of a Panamanian sharing a personal story about visiting a historical landmark or participating in a cultural tradition. For example, a story about visiting the Panama Canal, where a relative worked, or attending a local festival in a traditional village.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing personal experiences and opinions about cultural heritage, demonstrating understanding of different cultural perspectives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (oral answers):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What was the main topic of the speaker's story?" (e.g., A visit to a historical site and a personal connection.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What made the experience so special or important to the speaker?" (e.g., A family member's legacy, the cultural significance of the place.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which target vocabulary words did you hear the speaker use to describe the experience?" (e.g., 'pride', 'history', 'legacy', 'traditional', 'important', 'cultural').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking, using relative clauses ("which," "where"), Past Simple vs. Present Perfect, Future Simple for predictions. Vocabulary: heritage, history, sites, tourists, culture, restoration, villages, landmark, legacy, identity, efforts, preserve, attract, became, transformed, continue, traditional, global, critical, significant. Pronunciation: /eɪ/ sound, full/reduced vowels.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Builder (Controlled Practice):</b> In pairs, students receive sentence starters and word banks with target vocabulary and grammar cues (e.g., "Casco Viejo... (which)... tourist..."). They take turns orally completing sentences using relative clauses.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "El Valle de Antón is a beautiful place _______ many people go for relaxation." (Expected: "where")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "The Panama Canal, _______ opened in 1914, transformed global trade." (Expected: "which")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher monitors for correct grammar and pronunciation of words like 'great', 'trade', 'heritage'.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Story Chain (Semi-controlled Practice):</b> Students sit in small groups (3-4). The teacher starts a sentence about a cultural experience using Past Simple or Present Perfect (e.g., "I visited the Panama Canal last year..."). The first student adds a sentence using a relative clause ("...which is an incredible engineering landmark..."). The next student continues the story, potentially adding a prediction using Future Simple ("...we will continue to preserve its legacy for generations."). Students must integrate target vocabulary like 'heritage,' 'legacy,' 'preserve,' 'traditional,' and practice /eɪ/ and multisyllabic word pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interview & Peer Check:</b> Students work in new pairs. Partner A asks Partner B questions about cultural significance or historical sites, using pragmatic functions like: "Why is preserving historic landmarks like Casco Viejo or the Panama Viejo ruins important?" Partner B responds, describing a historical site or tradition using relative clauses and contrasting Past Simple/Present Perfect (e.g., "I visited the Panama Canal, where my grandfather worked during its construction. It has always been a source of great pride for my family."). Partner A provides feedback on clarity, grammar, and pronunciation.</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Preserving Our Heritage".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired Storytelling Exchange.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. Each student prepares to narrate a personal story (2-3 minutes) about an experience with a cultural tradition or a visit to a significant historical site in Panama. They should explain the personal and cultural importance of the experience for heritage preservation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They must incorporate at least two relative clauses ("which," "where"), use a mix of Past Simple and Present Perfect tenses, and include predictions with Future Simple regarding the future of heritage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students are encouraged to use specific vocabulary from the list (e.g., 'heritage,' 'legacy,' 'traditional,' 'preserve,' 'identity,' 'efforts'). They also need to practice the pronunciation of /eɪ/ and multisyllabic words, aiming for clear and intelligible speech.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Partner A narrates their story first. Partner B actively listens, showing interest (Sociolinguistic: "Hearing about traditional festivals from the Ngäbe-Buglé region made me want to participate...") and asks clarifying questions, then provides respectful feedback. Then, they switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example opening: "During Corpus Christi celebrations in La Villa de Los Santos, I watched traditional dances, <b>which</b> showcased our folkloric <b>heritage</b>. I <b>have always loved</b> this festival because it connects us to our <b>history</b>, and I believe we <b>will continue to preserve</b> these traditions for future generations."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Observation Checklist and Teacher Observation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> As students perform their storytelling exchange (Stage 4), peers use a simple checklist to provide feedback. The teacher circulates and observes, making notes on student performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Narrated a story about a personal experience.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used at least two relative clauses ("which," "where").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Used Past Simple and Present Perfect correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Included at least 3-4 target vocabulary words (e.g., 'heritage', 'preserve', 'traditional', 'legacy').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Spoke with intelligible pronunciation (especially /eɪ/ and multisyllabic words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explained the cultural significance and importance of heritage.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Showed interest and engaged respectfully during listening.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket (Oral response or quick written note):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of telling your story felt easiest today, and why?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which new grammar point (relative clauses, past simple/present perfect) did you use most effectively in your story?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection (Oral/Discussion):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel now about narrating personal experiences related to cultural heritage in English?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What's one thing you would like to improve about your speaking regarding describing historical sites or traditions?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The storytelling exchange allowed most students to practice relative clauses and past tenses effectively. The pronunciation focus on /eɪ/ and multisyllabic words needs continued reinforcement, perhaps through targeted drills. Some students struggled with differentiating Past Simple and Present Perfect when narrating sequences of events. For next time, I need to ensure more explicit modeling of scenarios requiring both tenses. The engagement with the theme was high due to the personal nature of the stories.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The personal stories students narrated today will serve as excellent material for the upcoming writing lesson (Lesson 4). Students will be asked to transform their oral narratives into written paragraphs, applying the same grammar structures and vocabulary, focusing on written accuracy and coherence.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>